+{
+ "cells": [
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "<a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/emijlop/master_thesis/blob/main/notebooks/02_identity_probes.ipynb\" target=\"_parent\"><img src=\"https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg\" alt=\"Open In Colab\"/></a>\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# 02 · Identity-dimension probes: which dimension drives each cluster?\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**Idea.** Train one linear probe per identity dimension on **frozen LaBSE** embeddings of a **community-built** benchmark (HESEIA). Each probe learns a direction in embedding space for one dimension (age, gender, disability, race/ethnicity, …). Projecting the items of another benchmark (BBQ, from notebook 01) onto these directions gives every cluster a profile across dimensions. The highest-scoring dimension is the one that, from the community's point of view, *drives* that cluster.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**How it connects to the research questions**\n",
+    "\n",
+    "| Output | Question it informs |\n",
+    "|---|---|\n",
+    "| Probe quality per dimension (§3) | Is each community-defined dimension linearly represented in LaBSE at all? A precondition for everything else |\n",
+    "| Similarity between probe directions (§4) | Structural validity: do e.g. gender and sexual orientation share a direction or stay separate? |\n",
+    "| Transfer to BBQ's own labels (§5) | RQ2: does BBQ's \"gender\" match HESEIA's \"gender\"? Low transfer means divergent operationalizations, or a language/format shift (see controls) |\n",
+    "| Cluster driver vs. BBQ's own category (§6) | RQ1/RQ2: a BBQ cluster labelled SES but driven by HESEIA's \"profession\" or \"race\" dimension |\n",
+    "| Co-activation on intersectional items (§7) | Additive vs. co-constitutive intersectionality |\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**Framing:** HESEIA labels are a *reference frame*, not ground truth. They are one systematization of the background concepts (contestedness), chosen deliberately because they come from the community. Every result reads as \"*from HESEIA's standpoint*, this BBQ cluster is driven by …\"."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "### Design decisions (also in `docs/decision_log.md`)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**1. Frozen LaBSE + linear probe (main analysis), not fine-tuning.**\n",
+    "- Fine-tuning LaBSE on identity labels would reshape the embedding space around HESEIA's categories. Clustering in that space would be **circular**: clusters would align with categories because the model was trained to make them align. The proposal's frozen-encoder argument (§4.2.1, step 3) depends on this not happening.\n",
+    "- Linear probes are the standard way to ask \"is concept X represented in these embeddings?\". They are interpretable: a weight vector is a direction you can project onto. Following Hewitt & Liang (2019), each probe is reported with a **control task** (same probe, shuffled labels), so high accuracy can't come from the probe memorizing.\n",
+    "- Fine-tuning appears only as an optional comparison in §8. Its embeddings are **never** used for the clustering of notebook 01.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**2. Train on HESEIA alone, not HESEIA + WinoQueer mixed.**\n",
+    "- If positives for one dimension came from one dataset (WinoQueer → sexual orientation) and negatives from another, the probe would learn *which dataset* an item comes from (language, format, template), not the dimension.\n",
+    "- WinoQueer is also highly templated (11 template sentences, 9 queer identity groups, 60 names, 182 predicates) and covers only LGBTQ+ identities. It is better used as a **held-out check** for a sexual-orientation / gender-identity probe: does a HESEIA-trained probe recognize WinoQueer items?\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**3. Explicit criteria for when results can be interpreted**, fixed before looking at cluster results:\n",
+    "- a dimension is used only if its cross-validated **selectivity ≥ 0.2** and **AUC ≥ 0.75** on HESEIA (§3);\n",
+    "- for interpreting BBQ clusters, additionally **transfer AUC ≥ 0.70** on BBQ's own harmonized labels (§5).\n",
+    "These thresholds are a choice; record any change in the decision log."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "# Setup (Colab or local), as in notebook 01\n",
+    "import sys, subprocess\n",
+    "from pathlib import Path\n",
+    "if \"google.colab\" in sys.modules and not Path(\"master_thesis\").exists():\n",
+    "    subprocess.run([\"git\", \"clone\", \"-q\", \"https://github.com/emijlop/master_thesis.git\"], check=True)\n",
+    "    subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"pip\", \"install\", \"-q\", \"-e\", \"master_thesis[pipeline]\"], check=True)\n",
+    "for p in [Path(\"master_thesis/src\"), Path(\"../src\")]:\n",
+    "    if p.exists(): sys.path.insert(0, str(p.resolve()))\n",
+    "\n",
+    "import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
+    "from benchbias import SEED, ENCODER, heseia, identity_probes as ip\n",
+    "from benchbias.embed import embed\n",
+    "\n",
+    "OUT = Path(\"outputs\"); OUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
+    "HESEIA_CSV = Path(\"data/raw/heseia/heseia.csv\")   # MODIFY: path to the downloaded CSV\n",
+    "\n",
+    "MIN_AUC, MIN_SELECTIVITY, MIN_TRANSFER_AUC = 0.75, 0.2, 0.70"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## 1. Load HESEIA and harmonize its labels\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Download the CSV from the Mozilla Data Collective (\"HESEIA Sentence Bias Dataset\") into `data/raw/heseia/`. Run `inspect_heseia` once, then set the column names and label map in `src/benchbias/heseia.py` (or pass them here). Native labels without a mapping keep their own name. HESEIA-only dimensions such as geographic origin or profession are exactly what RQ3 is about, so they become their own probes rather than being forced into a BBQ category."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "heseia.inspect_heseia(HESEIA_CSV)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "COLUMNS = {\"text\": None, \"labels\": None, \"group\": None}   # MODIFY after inspection\n",
+    "LABEL_MAP = {}                                            # MODIFY: native label -> harmonized construct\n",
+    "\n",
+    "H = heseia.load_heseia(HESEIA_CSV, columns=COLUMNS, label_map=LABEL_MAP)\n",
+    "print(H.shape, \"| intersectional share:\", H[\"intersectional\"].mean().round(3))\n",
+    "Y = ip.multi_hot(H[\"dimensions\"])\n",
+    "Y.sum().sort_values(ascending=False)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "# Co-occurrence of dimensions in HESEIA: which intersections did the community write about?\n",
+    "co = Y.T @ Y\n",
+    "co.where(~np.eye(len(co), dtype=bool)).fillna(0).astype(int)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## 2. Embed with frozen LaBSE"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "X = embed(H[\"text\"], ENCODER, cache=OUT / \"emb_heseia_LaBSE.npy\")\n",
+    "groups = H[\"group\"].to_numpy() if H[\"group\"].notna().all() else None\n",
+    "X.shape, (\"grouped CV\" if groups is not None else \"no group column: plain K-fold (check near-duplicates!)\")"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## 3. Probe quality: is each dimension represented, beyond what the probe could memorize?"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "quality = ip.evaluate_probes(X, Y, groups=groups, seed=SEED)\n",
+    "quality[\"usable\"] = (quality[\"AUC\"] >= MIN_AUC) & (quality[\"selectivity\"] >= MIN_SELECTIVITY)\n",
+    "quality.to_csv(OUT / \"probe_quality_heseia.csv\")\n",
+    "quality.sort_values(\"AUC\", ascending=False)"
